> [!NOTE] Status
> The graph surgery on this page is built and tested, and the listing below is real. What's missing
> is the statistics: nothing yet computes the marginal precision, so the example cannot produce a
> correct estimate ([What exists today](#what-exists-today)).

[The pose example](../example-pose-estimation/) solved for a whole trajectory at once. A robot
running for hours can't do that: the problem grows with every frame. A **sliding-window smoother**
keeps only the most recent poses, and when a pose leaves the window it is not simply deleted,
which would throw its information away. It is **marginalized**: what it said about the poses that
remain is kept as a single Gaussian factor over them.

This is where a model that can be edited in place pays off. The graph shrinks as fast as it grows,
and no node, term or binding leaks.

## One slide of the window

<ModelGraph spec={windowBefore} />

When $\pose_0$ leaves, its Markov blanket is $\pose_1$: the only remaining pose it touches. At the
current estimate, write the window's information over the retiring pose $\retiring$ and the blanket
$\blanket$ in blocks. The information that $\blanket$ keeps is the Schur complement:

$$
\Lam =
\begin{bmatrix} \Lam_{\retiring\retiring} & \Lam_{\retiring\blanket} \\ \Lam_{\blanket\retiring} & \Lam_{\blanket\blanket} \end{bmatrix}
\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
\tilde\Lam_{\blanket} = \Lam_{\blanket\blanket} - \Lam_{\blanket\retiring} \Lam_{\retiring\retiring}^{-1} \Lam_{\retiring\blanket} .
$$

It enters the model as a joint factor over $\blanket$, linearized at the frozen estimate
$\hat\pose_1$:

$$
-\log \tilde p(\pose_1) = \tfrac12 \left\lVert W (\pose_1 \ominus \hat\pose_1) \right\rVert^2 + \text{const},
\qquad W^\top W = \tilde\Lam_{\blanket} .
$$

This is exact for a linear-Gaussian model and a Laplace approximation otherwise.

<ModelGraph spec={windowAfter} />

## The graph surgery, as it runs today

This is the loop body from the sliding-window test. Each tick adds a state and its measurement, then
slides the window in three moves:

```rust
{../../../../crates/skoro-normal/tests/window.rs:117..142}
```

The test holds the factor count, the row count of $J$, and the graph's node count exactly steady
over twelve ticks. A leak or a dangling binding would be invisible in one tick and unmissable over
twelve.

> [!WARNING] What the test does not do
> It passes a fixed constant `whitening` where $W$ should be. The bookkeeping is right, but the
> statistics aren't there yet.

